Insertion Sort

Insertion Sort builds the sorted array one element at a time.

Think of arranging playing cards in your hand: you pick one card and insert it into its correct position.

Example
[5, 3, 8, 4, 2]

Goal:

[2, 3, 4, 5, 8]
How It Works

We consider the first element as already sorted.

[5 | 3, 8, 4, 2]
 ↑
sorted
Step 1: Insert 3

Compare 3 with 5.

5 > 3

Move 5 right and insert 3:

[3, 5 | 8, 4, 2]
Step 2: Insert 8

8 is greater than 5, so it stays:

[3, 5, 8 | 4, 2]
Step 3: Insert 4

Compare backwards:

4 < 8 → shift 8
4 < 5 → shift 5
4 > 3 → insert here

Result:

[3, 4, 5, 8 | 2]
Step 4: Insert 2

Move larger elements right:

[2, 3, 4, 5, 8]

Sorted! ✅

Python Code
def insertion_sort(arr):
    for i in range(1, len(arr)):
        key = arr[i]
        j = i - 1

        while j >= 0 and arr[j] > key:
            arr[j + 1] = arr[j]
            j -= 1

        arr[j + 1] = key

    return arr


arr = [5, 3, 8, 4, 2]

print(insertion_sort(arr))
Output
[2, 3, 4, 5, 8]
Understanding the Code
Step 1
key = arr[i]

Store the current element we want to insert into the correct position.

Example:

key = 4
Step 2
j = i - 1

Start comparing with the previous element.

Step 3
while j >= 0 and arr[j] > key:

As long as:

We are inside the array.
The previous element is greater than key.

Move the previous element one position right.

arr[j + 1] = arr[j]
Step 4

When the correct position is found:

arr[j + 1] = key

Insert the key.

Dry Run

Array:

[5, 3, 8, 4, 2]
Pass	Key	Array After Insertion
Start	—	[5, 3, 8, 4, 2]
1	3	[3, 5, 8, 4, 2]
2	8	[3, 5, 8, 4, 2]
3	4	[3, 4, 5, 8, 2]
4	2	[2, 3, 4, 5, 8]
Time Complexity
Case	Time Complexity
Best Case	O(n)
Average Case	O(n²)
Worst Case	O(n²)
Space Complexity
O(1)

Insertion Sort works in-place.

When Is Insertion Sort Good?

Insertion Sort works well when:

The array is small.
The array is already nearly sorted.
Only a few elements are out of order.

Example:

[1, 2, 3, 5, 4, 6]

It can sort this efficiently because only one element is misplaced.

Insertion Sort vs Other Sorts
Algorithm	Best	Average	Worst	Space
Bubble Sort	O(n)	O(n²)	O(n²)	O(1)
Selection Sort	O(n²)	O(n²)	O(n²)	O(1)
Insertion Sort	O(n)	O(n²)	O(n²)	O(1)
Key Takeaways
Insertion Sort builds a sorted portion from left to right.
Each element is inserted into its correct position.
Larger elements are shifted to the right.
It works well for small or nearly sorted arrays.
Best case: O(n).
Worst case: O(n²).
Space complexity: O(1).
